{"name":"Timid Absinthe","hex":"#dadfb0","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"timid","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Lacking in courage or confidence."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/timid"},{"term":"absinthe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The herb absinthium Artemisia absinthium (grande wormwood); essence of wormwood. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Bitterness; sorrow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/absinthe"}],"entries":[{"title":"Absinthe","blocks":[{"text":"Absinthe is an anise-flavoured spirit derived from several plants, including the flowers and leaves of Artemisia absinthium, together with green anise, sweet fennel, and other medicinal and culinary herbs. Historically described as a highly alcoholic spirit, it is 45–74% ABV or 90–148 proof in the US. Absinthe traditionally has a natural green colour but may also be colourless. It is commonly referred to in historical literature as la fée verte 'the green fairy'. While sometimes casually referred to as a liqueur, absinthe is not traditionally bottled with sugar or sweeteners. Absinthe is traditionally bottled at a high level of alcohol by volume, but it is normally diluted with water before being consumed."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Absinthe-glass.jpg/330px-Absinthe-glass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absinthe","wikidata":"Q170210"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Absinthe-glass.jpg/330px-Absinthe-glass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Absinthe-glass.jpg/330px-Absinthe-glass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAbsinthe-glass.jpg"},{"srcs